 "title": "The Hammer Candle for Beginners: Shape, Meaning and a Real Bitcoin Example",
 "text": "This one candle can warn you that a fall may be ending. Traders call it the hammer. Today you learn its shape, its meaning, and how to use it. First, every candle shows four prices. The body runs from the open to the close. The thin lines are wicks. They show the highest and lowest price. Each candle covers one time block, like 1 hour or 1 day. A hammer has a small body near the top. Its lower wick is long, at least 2 times the body. Here is the full shape: small body, long lower wick. A long upper wick is a different candle, not a hammer. The long wick means sellers pushed the price down hard. But buyers pushed it back up before the candle closed. So the buyers won this battle. A hammer matters most at the end of a downtrend. The same shape after a rise is a warning, not a hammer. Here is a real Bitcoin candle from November 8. It fell to 75,640 dollars. Then it closed at 75,907 dollars. Small body on top, long lower wick: a real hammer. 5 candles later, Bitcoin was 1.4 percent lower. Never act on the hammer alone. Wait for the next candle to close higher. That is confirmation. Put your stop loss just below the wick. Then the most you can lose is small and known. A hammer is a clue, never a promise. This is education, not financial advice. Big players leave clues too. We track whale moves live. Watch live whale moves for free on MarketRadar Whale.",
